 {"id":"CF-192","title":"P3 \u2014 install-hermes.sh turns its own public-bind auth gate OFF when a listener already occupies the port: --apply prints PASS port / PASS status_probe / HERMES: PASS on a config with no basic_auth","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":3,"created_at":"2026-11-04T09:57:29Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T09:57:29Z","detail":null,"reasoning":"`scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh:251` runs `check_public_bind_auth` and the gateway start only inside `if ! (command -v ss && ss -tln | grep -q \":$PORT \" )`. When anything already listens on the port, that whole branch is skipped: the adapter certifies `PASS  port \u2014 listening on :10800` and `PASS  status_probe \u2014 /api/status -> 200` from a listener it did not install and never auth-checked, then exits 0 with `HERMES: PASS`. CF-071's 19-arm fixture set stubs `ss` as `exit 1` (`tests/selftest.sh:386`), so every config arm exercises the free-port branch only: the gate's own subject is untested exactly where the gate is disabled. A node re-installed on a host whose port still holds a leftover unauthenticated gateway therefore reads green from the adapter.\n\nBoundary: distinct from CF-071 (grades the config PARSER; all 19 of its arms run on a free port), CF-150 (no step stages `basic_auth` at all), CF-092/CF-093 (README status rows are enforced by nothing). This row grades the CONDITION that disables CF-071's gate.","acceptance_criteria":"0. AC0 \u2014 reproduce at the filing tip from the repo root: the `probe` field prints the four lines under *today* (`PASS  port \u2014 listening on :10800`, `PASS  status_probe \u2014 /api/status -> 200`, `HERMES: PASS`, `auth_refusal_rows=0`) and exits 0. Re-derive the numbers at your own tip; do not copy the four lines as a fixed expectation of the fix.\n1. AC1 \u2014 a `tests/selftest.sh` arm drives the occupied-port branch: with a listener stub answering on `:PORT` and a config carrying NO `basic_auth`, `--apply` must NOT print `HERMES: PASS`. It must print either the existing auth refusal or a NAMED row stating the listener was not started by this adapter and its auth is unverified (e.g. `  FAIL  port \u2014 listening on :10800 (not started by this adapter; auth unverified)`), and exit nonzero. The arm's red proof is the pre-fix tree; the green proof is the fixed tree, both printed.\n2. AC2 \u2014 the same arm keeps the two existing green paths green in one run: free port + valid `basic_auth` still reaches `PASS gateway_start \u2014 /api/status -> 200`; free port + no `basic_auth` still prints the exact refusal wording CF-071 pinned.\n3. AC3 \u2014 `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints `SELFTEST: PASS` and `bash scripts/boardctl validate` prints `RESULT: OK` at the landing commit, and the fix is pushed with `git rev-list --count origin/main..HEAD` = 0.","probe":"d=$(mktemp -d); mkdir -p \"$d/bin\" \"$d/h/.hermes\" \"$d/h/hermes-venv/bin\"; printf '%s\\n' '#!/bin/sh' 'echo \"hermes 0.19.0\"' > \"$d/bin/hermes\"; printf '%s\\n' '#!/bin/sh' 'if [ \"$1\" = --version ]; then echo \"Python 3.12.3\"; exit 0; fi' 'exec python3 \"$@\"' > \"$d/bin/python\"; printf '%s\\n' '#!/bin/sh' 'echo \"LISTEN 0 4096 0.0.0.0:10800 0.0.0.0:*\"' > \"$d/bin/ss\"; printf '%s\\n' '#!/bin/sh' 'echo 200' > \"$d/bin/curl\"; chmod +x \"$d/bin/\"*; cp \"$d/bin/hermes\" \"$d/h/hermes-venv/bin/hermes\"; cp \"$d/bin/python\" \"$d/h/hermes-venv/bin/python\"; printf '%s\\n' 'dashboard:' '  port: 10800' > \"$d/h/.hermes/config.yaml\"; printf '%s\\n' 'DEMO_API_KEY=x' > \"$d/h/.hermes/.env\"; out=$(env PATH=\"$d/bin:$PATH\" HOME=\"$d/h\" HERMES_PYTHON_BIN=\"$d/bin/python\" HERMES_PIN=0.19.0 NODE_GATEWAY_PORT=10800 bash scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh --apply 2>&1); printf '%s\\n' \"$out\" | grep -E 'port \u2014|status_probe|HERMES:|needs dashboard'; echo \"auth_refusal_rows=$(printf '%s\\n' \"$out\" | grep -c 'needs dashboard.basic_auth')\"; rm -rf \"$d\""}
 {"id":"CF-193","title":"P2 — the public-bind auth gate refuses every config carrying ordinary YAML (a list item, a flow collection, a block scalar) and reports it as 'block absent', so a node with a valid basic_auth block cannot start its gateway","status":"pending","priority":"P2","complexity":3,"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-04 by the code-factory-duckbrain-qa lane (tick code-factory-duckbrain-qa-2026-11-04-09-00-46) at origin/main e0e73bd, re-verifying the foreman-closed row CF-071.\n\nWHERE THE GATE IS. scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh:66-188 is check_public_bind_auth(), a stdlib-only \"strict YAML subset\" reader introduced by CF-071's worker round 779ab4f to avoid a PyYAML dependency. mapping() (:139-156) accepts only `key: value` lines at an exact indent; every other construct raises ValueError, and :174-175 turns that into `refuse(\"block absent (invalid YAML)\")` — the whole file is discarded, including a perfectly good dashboard block.\n\nMEASURED, three arms, each file carrying a VALID non-empty dashboard.basic_auth block plus ONE ordinary YAML construct (staged at /home/kara/.hermes/cache/scratch/cfqa-cfbrain-20261104-090046.mhMpIP/probe2.sh, adapter fixture identical in shape to tests/selftest.sh::public-bind-auth):\n- `providers:` with one list item (`  - name: alpha`) -> `FAIL  gateway_start — refused: a public bind needs dashboard.basic_auth in ~/.hermes/config.yaml — block absent (invalid YAML)`, launch not reached.\n- `tools: [read_file, terminal]` (flow collection) -> the same refusal, launch not reached.\n- `prompt: |` (block scalar) -> the same refusal, launch not reached.\nControl in the same fixture: the identical config with none of the three constructs reaches the launch path (`PASS  gateway_start — /api/status -> 200`).\n\nMEASURED ON A REAL CONFIG. The operator's live ~/.hermes/config.yaml on this host (1191 lines, `dashboard.basic_auth` present at line 536, 303 list lines, 13 flow collections) is refused by the same gate with the same `block absent (invalid YAML)` reason, launch not reached — i.e. the refusal tells an operator the block is absent while it is present and non-empty.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS. specs/HARNESS.md:20 names this adapter as the only MEASURED harness and `install-hermes.sh --apply` as the node's gateway step, so this is the path every node takes. Any node whose config carries a provider list, a tool list or a flow collection — which is what every configured Hermes install has — can never reach gateway_start, and the failure misdirects the operator to a missing block that exists. It also violates AGENTS.md rule 3 in the sharpest form: the row prints a measured value that was never measured.\n\nWHY CF-071 DID NOT CATCH IT. Its selftest arms (tests/selftest.sh:346-366, 20 configs x 2 interpreter modes) are mapping-only fixtures; not one carries a construct a real config contains, so the strict reader's whole failure surface is invisible to the suite. It is distinct from CF-150 (nothing in the create path stages a config at all), from CF-191 (verdict exit codes) and from CF-071 itself (substring vs parse); this row is about what the parse accepts.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce on the current tree: stage a throwaway HOME whose ~/.hermes/config.yaml carries a valid non-empty dashboard.basic_auth block PLUS one `providers:` list item (`  - name: alpha`), stub `hermes --version` as 0.19.0 and symlink it into `$HOME/hermes-venv/bin/hermes`, then run `bash scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh --apply` and grep the gateway_start row: it prints `— block absent (invalid YAML)` and the launch path is not reached, while the same file without the list item reaches the launch path.\n1. A config whose dashboard block is present and non-empty is ACCEPTED for each of the three constructs measured here (list item, flow collection, block scalar): the run reaches the launch path and never prints a `block absent` reason.\n2. If a construct is still unsupported, the refusal names the construct and the 1-based line number that carries it, and the string `block absent` appears only for a file that really has no dashboard.basic_auth key — `grep -c 'block absent'` on the output of the list arm is 0.\n3. The existing distinct reasons survive unchanged: a comment-only config says `commented`, an empty/partial credential block says `empty value (username and password must both be non-empty)`, and a config with no dashboard key at all says `block absent`.\n4. tests/selftest.sh carries one named arm per construct (list, flow, block scalar) in the public-bind-auth section, plus a red control proving the fix is not always-accept: the same list-bearing file WITHOUT a basic_auth block is still refused, and a stub that accepts everything fails the suite. The section census in docs/ACCEPTANCE-PLAN.md is updated in the same change.\n5. `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints `SELFTEST: PASS` at the closing tip, run from a clean worktree of that tip (measured at e0e73bd before the fix: 43/43 CF-071 rows PASS, no `Bad substitution` line).","probe":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T10:01:41Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T10:01:41Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"79f37acf43c2b55d5e29625afb7cb5b254f5f506e7f386a6f49523beaf7ebf27"}}
 {"id":"CF-194","title":"P3 — the public-bind auth gate reads $HOME/.hermes/config.yaml while the gateway it launches resolves its data root from HERMES_HOME: it authorizes a file the server will not load and refuses a node that is correctly configured","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":2,"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-04 by the code-factory-duckbrain-qa lane (tick code-factory-duckbrain-qa-2026-11-04-09-00-46) at origin/main e0e73bd, re-verifying the foreman-closed row CF-071.\n\nTHE MISMATCH. scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh:69 hands check_public_bind_auth a hardcoded path, `\"$HOME/.hermes/config.yaml\"`, and :253 launches `nohup \"$VENV/bin/hermes\" serve --host 0.0.0.0 --port \"$PORT\" --skip-build` with no config argument and the caller's environment inherited. Hermes does not resolve its data root from `$HOME/.hermes` unconditionally: hermes_constants.py:194-201 defines get_hermes_home() as context override -> `HERMES_HOME` env -> platform default, and get_process_hermes_home() (:243-252) reads `HERMES_HOME` live; the served config is `<data root>/config.yaml` (hermes_cli/cli_config_load.py:265, `config_path = _hermes_home / 'config.yaml'`). So the gate can authorize one file while the server loads another.\n\nMEASURED, adapter fixture identical in shape to tests/selftest.sh::public-bind-auth (staged: /home/kara/.hermes/cache/scratch/cfqa-cfbrain-20261104-090046.mhMpIP/probe.sh):\n- Arm A (control, $HOME config with a real block): `PASS  gateway_start — /api/status -> 200`, launch reached, credentials leaked: 0.\n- Arm B ($HOME config with a real block, `HERMES_HOME` naming a directory whose config.yaml is `dashboard: {}`): `PASS  gateway_start — /api/status -> 200`, launch reached with `HERMES_HOME=<alt-unauth>`, and the product's own resolver under the same environment prints `resolved_hermes_home=<alt-unauth>`, `config_has_basic_auth_block=False`. The gate certified a file the launched gateway will not read — exactly the condition CF-071 exists to refuse.\n- Arm C (`HERMES_HOME` naming a directory whose config.yaml has the valid block, `$HOME/.hermes/config.yaml` comment-only): `FAIL  gateway_start — refused: … — commented`, launch NOT reached; the resolver names the alt directory with `config_has_basic_auth_block=True`. A correctly configured node is refused.\n\nNOT HYPOTHETICAL. The control arm's launch marker recorded `HERMES_HOME=/home/kara/.hermes` — the variable is exported in this fleet host's ambient environment (the adapter inherits it), and this host carries three profile data roots under ~/.hermes/profiles/ (eduos-e2e-tester, uncensored, voice), so a profile-scoped node's served config is a different file from the one the gate reads.\n\nWHY CF-071 DID NOT CATCH IT. Every arm of its fixture sets HOME and none sets HERMES_HOME (tests/selftest.sh:427-430), so the two paths can never diverge inside the suite; the row's acceptance criteria are about the content of one file, never about which file the product reads.\n\nCAVEAT, stated so the fix is not overclaimed: the resolver measured here is the host's Hermes build (v0.22.5), while the adapter pins 0.19.0. The row's criterion is therefore about the contract (the gate must read what the launched process reads) and about the adapter's own measured behaviour, not about a claim that 0.19.0 resolves identically.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce both arms in one throwaway fixture (stub `hermes --version` = 0.19.0, symlinked as `$HOME/hermes-venv/bin/hermes`): (a) `$HOME/.hermes/config.yaml` with a real basic_auth block and `HERMES_HOME` naming a directory whose config.yaml is `dashboard: {}` still prints `PASS  gateway_start`, and the launch marker records `HERMES_HOME=<that directory>`; (b) the reverse pair ($HOME comment-only, HERMES_HOME with the real block) prints `FAIL gateway_start — refused: … — commented` and does not reach launch.\n1. The gate reads the config file the launched process will read: with `HERMES_HOME` set, the evaluated path is `$HERMES_HOME/config.yaml`; with it unset, `$HOME/.hermes/config.yaml`. The resolved path appears verbatim in the gate's own row (both PASS and FAIL forms, e.g. `— config <path>`) so an operator can see which file was graded.\n2. Arm (a) is REFUSED and names the resolved path plus the reason (no basic_auth in that file); arm (b) reaches the launch path. Both are recorded in the evidence text with the resolver output that names the data root.\n3. A node whose `HERMES_HOME` points at a directory that does not exist, or whose config is unreadable there, is refused with a reason that names the path — never a silent fallback to `$HOME/.hermes`.\n4. tests/selftest.sh carries both arms as named rows in the public-bind-auth section plus a red mutation: a gate that hardcodes `$HOME/.hermes/config.yaml` again must make the suite fail, not pass.\n5. `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints `SELFTEST: PASS` at the closing tip from a clean worktree of that tip.","probe":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T10:01:41Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T10:01:41Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"b33c64b110def123916ed79e9a7481d6157c27485244cacf0c79a16f04dc6a4e"}}
+{"id":"PERF-016","title":"P3 — resolve the git binary once instead of PATH-probing it on every spawn: 2612 execve attempts per lane-target run, 2294 of them ENOENT PATH probes (-13% unit / -11% gate at load 11-21; effect NOT resolvable at load 6-10)","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":3,"reasoning":"Drafted by perf fire 2026-11-03 18-55-30, re-validated by 2026-11-03 19-51-49, 2026-11-04 01-21-15, 2026-11-04 03-41-13 and 2026-11-04 08-12-52 (series: ~/.hermes/stand-in/perf/code-factory/perf-2026-11-04-evidence.md). Attributed with a PATH shim and strace: the 77 `git` invocations per lane-target run are launched as bare `git` in argv, so CPython's execvp probes every PATH entry before the real binary — 2294 of 2401-2612 execve attempts in the run are ENOENT probes. Change: module-level `shutil.which(\"git\")` cache in `scripts/lane_target.py::_git` and `tests/test_lane_target.py::_git`, two files, no output/assertion/exit-code change. Measured effect by load regime: load 11-21 -> unit 1.740->1.513 s and gate 8.423->7.460 s CPU (-13% / -11%, resolvable); load 38-47 -> -8.7% / -2.8% (overlapping); load 7.4-8.4 -> -6.9% / -2.3% (unit not resolvable); load 6.3-10.4 -> -3.6% / +0.4% (not resolvable). So the CPU claim is regime-dependent and the deterministic claim is the execve/ENOENT count. This row was re-filed with corrected criteria by tick 2026-11-04-09-53-51, whose first filing wrongly proposed the memoised two_roots() candidate that the 08-12-52 fire had already refuted (spawns -21%, CPU unchanged) — the id was right, the candidate was wrong.","acceptance_criteria":"1. Paired CPU measurement of `tests/test_lane_target.py` (base vs patch, three runs each, alternated back-to-back) inside ONE quoted load regime, and the same for the whole gate (`bash tests/selftest.sh`). Require non-overlapping ranges before claiming a CPU win. State the regime in the row note and the audit doc. If the regime yields overlapping ranges (the fourth regime on record did: unit -3.6%, gate +0.4% at load 6.3-10.4), report the measured floor and DO NOT claim a CPU win — the resolvable result is then AC-4 only.\n2. `tests/test_lane_target.py` keeps 40 tests, the same test names, and prints `OK` (exit 0).\n3. Whole gate at the closing tip: `SELFTEST: PASS`, rc=0, 190 PASS rows at e0e73bd (re-count, do not copy), byte-identical PASS row text, no new SKIP row.\n4. Determinism arm (the invariant that survives any load regime): `strace -f -e trace=execve python3 -B tests/test_lane_target.py` shows <=500 execve attempts from a baseline >=2500 at e0e73bd, and <=200 of those are ENOENT PATH probes from a baseline of ~2294. Quote both counts in the audit doc.\n5. Numbers, commands, load regime, both directions of the claim (what was measured vs what was projected) and the honest remaining floor go in `docs/audits/code-factory-perf-2026-11-04-PERF-016.md`.\n6. Commit message carries AGENTS.md rule 2 and the `# ch:trace row=PERF-016` marker.","probe":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T10:01:33Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T10:01:33Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"735da7a1d56003f87e1079b2cc283c9498f7db4633b20849e9d3c019c40c4c42"}}
